{
  "requirement": "The review prompt MUST include at least one worked example of a mechanical change and at least one worked example of a design question. The mechanical example MUST show a flawed draft together with the change the reviewer makes to it.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement only constrains what the review prompt says. \"MUST include at least one worked example of a mechanical change and at least one worked example of a design question\" and \"The mechanical example MUST show a flawed draft together with the change the reviewer makes to it\" both describe document content. They do not prescribe libraries, types, signatures, delegation or algorithms. Any prompt layout that has those examples would meet them, so no clause is a structural obligation.\n\nThe root `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md`, in `section[Requirement group review]/section[Examples]`, also contains the content:\n- Mechanical example: a flawed compound draft is quoted (\"The `refactor` command accepts file, node, and definition mappings, ...\"). The reviewer's change follows it: split it into `mappings.file`, `mappings.node`, and the other listed requirements, and \"Not a question.\" A second mechanical wording fix is also given.\n- Design question example: a quoted note about `rewrite.rationale`, with options (a), (b) and (c) and a recommendation.\n\nNo further implementation evidence is needed.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has two behavioral obligations. (1) The review prompt includes a worked mechanical example, and that example shows a flawed draft together with the reviewer's change to it. (2) The prompt includes a worked design-question example.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_works_a_mechanical_example_and_a_design_question]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It extracts the `## Examples` section of the real prompt file with `section(review_prompt(), \"## Examples\")`. It then calls `assert_text_in_order`, which panics unless each phrase appears after the previous one.\n\n- **Mechanical example:** `Compound, with a mechanical fix:` introduces it. `> The \\`refactor\\` command accepts` is the quoted flawed draft. `Split into` is the change the reviewer makes. `Not a question` classifies it as mechanical. This covers the flawed draft together with the change.\n- **Design question:** `A design question:` introduces it. `Options:` and `Recommend` show a worked question with options and a recommendation.\n\nI read `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md#section[Requirement group review]/section[Examples]`. Its content matches these phrases: a quoted compound draft, a split with \"Not a question\", and a design question with options and a recommendation.\n\nThe ordered, section-scoped assertions would fail if the examples were removed, if the draft or its fix were dropped, or if the design question were missing or moved out of `## Examples`. A phrase-presence check cannot judge example quality. The requirement asks only for the examples' presence and shape, so that is enough. I found no unchecked obligation.",
  "references": "35fb59a6c679625c5f1fae56e193cb74da150ada08d8c6e27fb874bca37043a0",
